{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nEpic 62dc's criterion 5 is undelivered: plan review never tells a reviewer that the plan overlaps work another ticket describes. Each plan is read in isolation, so two tickets planning one change both pass. Create-time surfacing matches a title and short description where plan review matches a full plan, but one index serves both.\n\n## Approach\n\n**Reuse the index; add no second retrieval path.** The sibling story builds a lexical FTS5 index under `.rebar/cache/similar-tickets/`, caught up in the read phase. Its whole public surface is `find_similar` (`src/rebar/similar/__init__.py:46-54`), taking query text via `text=` (`:51`) and `exclude_id=` for self-hits (`:52`). With the optional embedding tier configured it ranks through that tier, else lexically (`:79-82`): no model provider is required.\n\n**The one gap: failure distinguishability.** Every failure returns `[]`, per \"Never raises\" (`:55`), so a caller cannot tell an absent index from a failed catch-up from an empty match.\n\n**Diagnostic contract.** `find_similar` gains one keyword-only parameter `diagnostics: dict | None = None`; given a dict it records a single `status`. The set is closed, one value per branch: `index-absent` `:63`, `catch-up-failed` `:66-71`, `retrieval-error` at the outer handler `:84`, else `ok` (`:80` embedding, `:82` lexical). An embedding-provider failure is NOT a status: `embedding.rank` returns `None` for every cause it cannot serve (`:76-79`), and `:82` then serves lexical results, so that path is `ok`. Return type, \"Never raises\" and every existing call stay unchanged: the signal travels through the dict. The only `src/` consumer, `recent_creates.py:196`, passes none.\n\n**Recording channel: coverage, written in place.** `run_pass1` takes `coverage` as a mutable dict (`pass1.py:238`) and assigns diagnostic keys directly -- e.g. `coverage[\"isf\"]` `:473`; `coverage[\"overlap\"]` is written the same way.\n\n**Ride the existing Pass-1 extra-context seam.** A Pass-1 finder unit already accepts store-derived `extra_context` (`checkpoints.py:54`), its identity binding that context by `context_digest`, a sha256 over the injected text (`:61-63`). That text is built by `_chunk_extra_context` (`pass1.py:215`), called at `:326`, so injection lands in `pass1.py`. The seam is already criterion-keyed -- `:219` injects G5 context when G5 is in the chunk, `:221` the no-file-impact block when that criterion is -- so overlap adds a third branch on that test, keyed to `project.plan-overlap`. Riding it rather than adding a pass leaves checkpoint identity and caching unchanged: a changed candidate set yields a different `context_digest`.\n\n**Zero marginal LLM cost: the existing Pass-1 unit does the judging.** Candidates ride a chunk already being sent, so judging adds no LLM call; lexical retrieval is not one either. The cap is on candidates, not calls: 5, the default `RESULT_LIMIT` (`lexical.py:39`), the operating point ADR 0122 makes load-bearing.\n\n**On demand, not on every review.** The switch is one keyword-only parameter `overlap: bool = False` on `review_plan` (`plan_review/__init__.py:199`), threaded exactly as `probe_criteria`, because `review_plan` assembles no batch request. The chain: `review_plan` -> `gate_dispatch.produce_plan_review_verdict` (`gate_dispatch.py:64`, called at `__init__.py:473`) -> `run_workflow` inputs (`:183`) -> a new `overlap:` input in `gates/plan-review.yaml` (`:24` declares `probe_criteria`) wired into the finders batch `with:` (`:54`, `:66`) -> `req.with_inputs` -> `ProductionBatchRunner.run` (`production_batch_runner.py:120-124`) -> `run_pass1` (`pass1.py:232`) -> `_chunk_extra_context`. Always threaded, `False` = off, so `${{ inputs.overlap }}` always resolves.\n\n**The flag gates FAN-IN, so the off switch is total.** For a PROJECT criterion the `include_project_plan_overlap` key (`workflow_ops.py:267`) is only a coverage record: `_project_criteria` never reads it, filtering solely on the `probe` allowlist (`production_batch_runner.py:283-287`, fed at `:123`). `overlap` therefore rides beside `probe` at `:120-124`, and `_keep` gains one arm dropping the id when false. Not fanned in, the id never reaches `registry.chunk_by_facet` (`pass1.py:262`), so with `overlap=False` it sits in no chunk's rubric and is absent from `prompt_id` (`checkpoints.py:66`): checkpoint identity stays byte-identical, and no finder can emit the finding from plan text alone.\n\n**Decision rule: posture, not prompt.** Mirroring `decomposition_state_block` (`det_lint.py:46`, shape at `:66-70`), `overlap.py` emits a `## POSSIBLE OVERLAP (from store)` heading, a directive to judge whether this plan does work a listed ticket describes, then one `alias -- title (similarity <score>)` line per candidate. Both backends return exactly `id`/`alias`/`title`/`score` (`similar/lexical.py:110-117`) and the index row (`:101`) carries no workflow status, so `score` is the only per-candidate signal available, and it is what separates a near-duplicate plan from a distant neighbour. Identity falls back to `id` when `alias` is absent. A new `project.plan-overlap` entry in `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` carries `exec: \"1-TURN\"`, `facet: \"project-invariants\"`, `default_posture: \"advisory\"` -- the shape `project.symbol-reference-completeness` has. `exec: \"1-TURN\"` attributes the finding: the fan-in above puts the id in a chunk's rubric, and `P1Finding.criteria` (`passes.py:51`) is the finder's rubric-id field, so it is tagged because it is IN the rubric, not because prose asks it. Posture then decides: the decide pass partitions findings by `decision` (`orchestrator.py:294-301`) and `_any_blocking_criterion` (`:325-330`) intersects a finding's `criteria` with blocking-posture ids only, keyed to `default_posture` (`det_invariants.py:85`), so an advisory id can never reach `blocking`.\n\n**Degradation.** On any non-`ok` status the candidate list is empty and no overlap finding is emitted; the review proceeds normally.\n\n**Injection site has little headroom, so work is extracted.** `pass1.py` is 658 lines against the 800 cap, so overlap retrieval and its diagnostic live in a new `plan_review/overlap.py`; `_chunk_extra_context` gains only the call.\n\nAny ratcheted mechanism added carries a `# mechanism-ok:` marker naming this ticket at its definition site.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] With `overlap=True`, reviewing a ticket whose plan restates an existing ticket's plan emits an advisory overlap finding naming that ticket; on a store seeded with over 5 matches exactly 5 candidates are injected. One seeded-store test per case.\n- [x] The off switch is total, one test per case: with `overlap=False` no overlap finding is emitted; `project.plan-overlap` is absent from `coverage[\"batch_resolution\"][\"single\"]`; checkpoint identity stays byte-identical to the same review before this change; with `overlap=True` the LLM call count equals the `overlap=False` count.\n- [x] An overlap finding can never turn a verdict from pass to block, one test per case: it is attributed to a criterion whose routing `default_posture` is `advisory`; the decide pass places it there; a passing plan's verdict is unchanged with one present.\n- [x] Two compatibility invariants, one test each: `find_similar` called without `diagnostics` returns what it did before this change (the `recent_creates.py:196` call); with no model provider configured overlap retrieval still returns lexical-tier candidates.\n- [x] Degradation, one test per status (`index-absent` absent index; `catch-up-failed` catch-up raises; `retrieval-error` retrieval raises): `coverage[\"overlap\"][\"status\"]` records it; the overlap finding set is empty; the verdict equals the same review with `overlap=False`.\n- [x] The tree-wide gates pass, one check each: every module added or modified under `src/rebar/` is at most 800 lines (module-size gate); `scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` exits 0; `scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` exits 0."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793721591463867328,"uuid":"09dadf17-065e-4c2e-acc4-3f084898bf6a"}